[#:seon.task{:name "an-agent-runs-its-proofs-with-clojure-test"
            :plan "At its REPL an agent runs a test namespace's deftests the Clojure way, and they run through its one test path. `(require 'seon.id-test)` enters the program namespace (`seon.sci.eval/agent-require` calls `seon.sci.eval/enter-namespace!` before `sci.impl.namespaces/require`; the base holds every test namespace empty, so SCI's require loaded nothing and `#'seon.id-test/...` was 'Unable to resolve var', chain run 2 agent chain2-forgive-2). `clojure.test/run-test-var`, `test-var`, `test-vars` and `run-tests` (`seon.sci.lookup/agent-test-vars`) run each test Var through `my.test/check`, which records its result on the agent's branch (`:seon.test.run/id`), and answer that `:seon.test/run-result` with the clojure.test summary counts; host `clojure.test/run-test-var` threw a ClassCastException on an SCI namespace. Read `seon.sci.lookup/agent-test-namespace`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "run-test-var-on-a-just-defined-test-refuses-its-own-output"
             :plan "In an agent's SCI context, `(clojure.test/run-test-var #'seon.cluster.host-test/an-ordinary-message-wakes-the-idle-hosted-agent)` right after redefining that deftest answered a contract error: `seon.sci.lookup/agent-test-vars` refused its result at `[:seon.error/at]` (a map missing `:seon.error/at`, `:seon.error/layer` and `:seon.error/operation`), called from `seon.sci.lookup/agent-run-test-var`. The producer builds an error map without the base members; build it with the error constructor (default 18919b663, 2026-10-10)."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-run-in-the-request-that-redefines-its-subject-misses-the-redefinition"
             :plan "Agent sci-tests-b on default a6cbe4fcf, 2026-10-10, sent one request: `(in-ns 'seon.program)`, then a `defn-` of `seon.program/edn-symbol-text?` answering false, then `(clojure.test/run-test-var #'seon.program-test/edn-symbol-text-agrees-with-the-reader-on-slash-led-text)`. The run answered `{:test 1 :pass 8 :fail 0}`. The automatic test step of the same request ran the same test red, and a `my.test/check` in the next request answered 5 pass and 3 fail. `seon.sci.lookup/agent-test-vars` calls `my.test/check`, whose member branches fork from the agent's committed head, and the request's own definitions are not committed until it records. So a test run in the request that redefines its subject runs against the old definition and answers green. Done when that run sees the redefinition, or answers that its subject changed in this request and lists it under `:my.test/after-recording`, as `my.test/check` does for a name defined earlier in the same request. Read `seon.test/run`."} #:seon.task{:name "an-agent-running-a-recorded-test-gets-its-counts"
             :plan "An agent's `clojure.test/run-test-var`, `test-vars` and `run-tests` (`seon.sci.lookup/agent-test-vars`) name the tests to run: `my.test/check` takes them as `:seon.test/identities` and runs exactly those through `seon.test/run`, answering a reusable test with its recorded counts, never `:seon.test/unreached` for a test the agent named. Today `agent-test-vars` passes them as `:seon.test/changed`, whose reach selects nothing once the test holds evidence on the branch (after the automatic test step ran it, or a first `run-test-var`): `my.test/check` answers `:seon.test/unreached` without `:seon.test/passed?` and `agent-test-vars` refuses its own output (agent ap-probe-1 on default 340462543, 2026-10-10, proof `seon.competency-test/t5-slug-removes-edge-hyphens`)."}
 #:seon.task{:name "doc-shows-the-arglists-the-defn-declares"
             :plan "`(doc seon.cluster.source/tested-task-basis)` shows `:arglists ([sym])` while the function takes `[main candidate base sym]` (lane s3b, 2026-10-14). The shown arglists come from `seon.sci.lookup/agent-arglists`, which drops the positional arguments that call preparation fills by schema name (`:seon.db/database-value` read from the bound connection), so the three database arguments disappear, although this function's three databases are DIFFERENT values (main, candidate, merge base) that the caller must pass, and a call with only `sym` is refused. Done when `doc` shows the full declared arglists for an argument that call preparation could fill but the function declares more than once under the same schema (the filled one marked or the supplied ones named), proven by a deftest over `seon.sci.lookup/agent-arglists` for `seon.cluster.source/tested-task-basis` and a one-database function (unchanged). Read `seon.sci.lookup/agent-arglists` and `seon.call-preparation/supplied-arguments`."}]
